From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: H. M. Yousuf <hmyousuf@nirapod.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 00:33:14 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] armv8a: add the bitwise selections BSL and BIT

BSL and BIT build each bit of a 128-bit result from one of two
registers, under the control of a third. Each gives one SHA-2 function
in a single instruction: Ch(e, f, g) is BIT(g, f, e) and Maj(a, b, c)
is BSL(b ^ c, a, b), where forms built from AND, BIC and EOR take
three instructions each.

Both read and write their destination, like the SHA-2 schedule and hash
updates. They're provided for the 16B arrangement only, as intrinsics
without a size suffix: d = #VBSL(d, n, m) and d = #VBIT(d, n, m). The
semantics transcribe the pseudocode of DDI 0487 M.d (C7.2.24 BIT,
C7.2.25 BSL), and both are data-independent-time instructions (C5.2.4).
armv8a_simd_lemmas.v states that they select bits as described, and
that BIT and BSL in the roles above compute Ch and Maj.

BIF, the third instruction of the group, isn't added. Its result is
that of BIT with the two data operands exchanged, or of BSL with the
control operand as destination, so it only changes which register is
overwritten. Nothing is lowered to the new instructions; they're
intrinsics only.

simd_exec.jazz runs both on fixed inputs in which every combination of
three bits occurs, through the compiler's semantics, against values
produced by the instructions on hardware.
